y to the transferrin  receptor  and this has been labeled with the fab  flurry agent  and after six hours the image on the  left hand side  you can see there's a very clear red  fluorescent signal within the cytoplasm  of these cells the image on the right  hand side is of  an igg isotype control that has been  labeled in a similar manner  and you can see there is no red  fluorescence present  and if we now do a quantification of  those images you can see there's a very  fast rapid increase in the red object  area in the well  which is not seen in the isotype or the  media control wells  and this shows it's a very specific  cytoplasmic localization and specific  for the target antibody  and we can also say there's a nice rapid  time dependent increase in the signal  now from the image you can see that  obviously the number of cells is very  dependent on the  red area that we can see within the well  so we went on to validate that  um the how the signal size was dependent  on cell number  so in this scenario here we've put an  increasing number of hd 1080s in our  well  we've then treated them again with our  cd71 that's labeled for fab floor  and as you can see with the graph in the  middle here as the number of cells is  increased in the well  there is a large increase in the red  object area within the well  and this is in line with the number of  cells that have been added  if we now use the red object area and  actually normalize this for the phase  area  as a surrogate for the identity of cells  in the well  we end up with the data on the right  hand side here which shows that although  the signal size is dependent on cell  number  the actual rate of internalization of  the antibody in producing the signal  is cell number independent  and this is quite a nice example from  this data to show  that there is a signal size number is  dependent but actually the rate is  independent  we also want to go on to show that the  response that we're looking at is a  specific response  so in this scenario here we've taken  gerkats which are a t  cell-like cell line and rarjes which  were a b-cell-like cell line  and tested them with a range of cd  surface marker antibodies  that have been labeled with the fab  flurry agent  and in both instances here you can see  both cell lines gave a nice response to  cd 71  which is a very ubiquitously expressed  receptor i mean that's the green line  here  and then you can see that both cell  lines also give us a nice response to  cd45  which is a general lymphocyte marker but  only the t  cells will give us response to the cd3  and  only the b cells give us a nice clear  response with the cd20 marker  and we can we can then produce a nice  expression profile for these  for these cell lines now you'll also  note  that those particular cell lines um  although they have the same have a  response to cd71  the rajis appear to give a faster and a  larger  response than the jerk hats and we feel  that this may be dependent on the level  of receptor expression or the ability of  those cell lines to actually internalize  the marker  but the data set here gives us the  expected cd surface marker expression  profile  and also demonstrates that the signal we  see here although specific  it does give us a generic utility for  labeling multiple antibodies  and the last piece of validation data  here is showing that the  the antibody internalization signal we  see is actually within the lysosome  compartment of the cell  so in the set up here we have got hd  1080 cells again that have been treated  with cd71  and the top image on the right hand side  here shows the nice red  antibody internalization signal we've  then added  lysosensor which is a lysosomal marker  which gives us a green signal and this  is shown in the bottom left hand corner  then using the inkyocite software we can  look at the coincidence of the two  red mar the two coincident of the two  marker  within the cell and this is shown in  that yellow  marking there and if we look at the  quantification of the images  we can see that about 74 of the red  area in these cells is co-localized with  the green  area so it supports that the presence of  the internalized fat fluid reagent  is within the lysosomal compartments  we're now going to change track a little  bit and consider  how a therapeutic antibody is actually  selected and optimized within a drug  discovery setting  so in most instances the first stage  is to raise your antibody and this is  normally done as multiple hybridomas and  here we're normally looking in the range  of a thousand antibodies  normally producing low quantities and  within a mouse a rabbit  or a rat and the first step then is to  check for binding or engagement of the  antibody with the target  and this is obviously often done with  binding  and we're looking at binding  characteristics normally of around 500  to 1000 antibodies  the next step is to test these for  functional affinity ranking  and this is where the first early  cellular testing is normally carried out  and this is probably the first stage in  this pathway  where the real-time antibody  internalization assay could offer  um be enabling so at this stage we would  be looking at single concentration  screening of probably hundreds of  antibodies  and because we can label those in the  serum containing media we can use the  non-purified  hybridoma samples to allow direct  comparison of these samples  the next stage is often to take those  antibodies of interest  scale them up and then do further  functional evaluation on them  and again the application we're talking  about here  would allow you to look at full  concentration range profiling of those  reagents and possibly to do a rate  comparison of them  the next stage is often to take those  key  antibodies of interest and then humanize  them  ready for a therapeutic application and  again  the inky site can offer the full  concentration range profiling  mechanistic